#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148301} :activity/id #uuid "483b4cdd-3e7f-59a6-bbea-e32b567334fc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049007} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148751 :tutorial/content "A **Bernoulli differential equation** is an ordinary differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}}+ p ( x ) y = q ( x ) y^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous functions, and $n$ is any real number.\n\nThe substitution $z=y^{1-n}$ reduces any Bernoulli equation to a first-order linear differential equation. \n\nFor example, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}}+y = x y^2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) =1, \\qquad q(x) = x, \\qquad n=2.\n$$\n\nTo reduce our equation to a linear differential equation, we first divide it by $y^2{:}$\n\n$$\ny^{-2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} +y^{-1} = x\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is now a function of $x$ only.\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} = y^{1-2} = y^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = -y^{-2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny^{-2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} +z  = x\n$$\n\nWriting the above in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} -z=-x.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use integrating factors to solve it." :tutorial/id #uuid "238cafad-c43b-5970-af70-5ce0cab5442c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4083 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "df4c3412-22c6-5e09-b51f-919927396ab5" :step/math-academy-id 24488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148324}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147453 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147390 :question/id #uuid "d1946905-5833-58c2-9a0a-4dfd04eb922b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8963" :question/problem "The substitution $z=y^m$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3, \\qquad q(x) = 1, \\qquad n=-\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny^{1/2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y^{3/2}= 1.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\dfrac{3}{2}y^{1/2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny^{1/2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(\\ast)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^3\\,z = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{3}{2}x^3z = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc2192f-24a4-5160-844c-4d17096779e5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048365}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186648605}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Bernoulli Differential Equation to First Order Linear"} :step/id #uuid "f524e832-62f5-581a-b2f3-1b3ea1960d06" :step/math-academy-id 33461 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148322}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147454 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147391 :question/id #uuid "047ea3c1-8ebf-5e26-9f28-c5a87e7cf997" :question/math-academy-id "e-8965" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x}  = \\dfrac{x^2}{y} .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z  = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z\n\t\t=2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2{x}^{2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =2{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2}) & =2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{2} & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}+C}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}.$ This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{ 2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520737} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520734}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "4e404a11-810d-553c-ba28-12be5a7f6dfc" :step/math-academy-id 33463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148324 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147393 :question/id #uuid "b5189ec2-ea1c-5036-9543-b963bd072ab7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9069" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- $y\\,y' + x^2y^2 = e^x y^4$\n\n- $xy' + x^3y = -x^2 y^3$\n\n- $y' + y\\cos{x} = e^{y+1}$" :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = e^x y^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) = e^x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = -xy^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) =-x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x)y^n$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef27d2b-6149-5070-b711-f4dcc7db2a03" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519913} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186648621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186648624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186648678}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bernoulli Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "df9ef366-3f66-55d8-9164-2ebeca5e2ff5" :step/math-academy-id 33831 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148321}}] :activity/title "Bernoulli Differential Equations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147701}]}
